Q: Writing an entire list to a file in java A: <h2>Modern Java</h2>
<p>Let's try using features of modern Java such as NIO.2, streams, lambdas, and method references. We will test elapsed time.</p>
<h2>Write file</h2>
<p>GGenerate one million UUID values, represented as text in canonical hexadecimal format.</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>List &lt; String &gt; uuids = IntStream.range( 0 , 1_000_000 ).mapToObj( index -&gt; UUID.randomUUID() ).map( UUID :: toString ).toList();
</code></pre>
<p>Write those to a file. We pass our list of <code>String</code> objects to <code>Files.write</code>.</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>try { Files.write( path , uuids ); }
catch ( IOException e ) { e.printStackTrace(); }
</code></pre>
<p>Data snippet:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>b541cd3c-60d2-4b11-b7da-7f21a5c18bea
a3446075-5bbd-42ab-a9b4-a154f8190900
9144b23b-de0e-41a8-8285-b0b0dd8ccbe6
…
</code></pre>
<p>You said you re-write the file by appending more data. Let's append a delimiter of <code>|</code> (pipe character) and the current moment. We manipulate the data in memory, then write back out to the same file.</p>
<p>Manipulate the data.</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>lines = lines.stream().map( line -&gt; line.concat( &quot;|&quot; ).concat( Instant.now().toString() ) ).toList();
</code></pre>
<p>Rewrite to the same file.</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>try { Files.write( path , lines ); }
catch ( IOException e ) { e.printStackTrace(); }
</code></pre>
<p>Pulling all that code together looks like the following.</p>
<pre class="lang-java prettyprint-override"><code>public class RewriteBigFile
{
    static void main ( )
    {
        IO.println( Runtime.version() );
        long start = System.nanoTime();

        // Input data. Generate one million UUID values, represented as text in canonical hexadecimal format.
        List &lt; String &gt; uuids = IntStream.range( 0 , 1_000_000 ).mapToObj( index -&gt; UUID.randomUUID() ).map( UUID :: toString ).toList();

        // Write file.
        final String fileName = &quot;/Users/basil_dot_work/example.txt&quot;;
        final Path path = Paths.get( fileName );

        try { Files.write( path , uuids ); }
        catch ( IOException e ) { e.printStackTrace(); }

        // Read file.
        List &lt; String &gt; lines;
        try { lines = Files.readAllLines( path ); }
        catch ( IOException e ) { throw new RuntimeException( e ); }

        // Manipulate data.
        lines = lines.stream().map( line -&gt; line.concat( &quot;|&quot; ).concat( Instant.now().toString() ) ).toList();

        // Rewrite file.
        try { Files.write( path , lines ); }
        catch ( IOException e ) { e.printStackTrace(); }

        Duration duration = Duration.ofNanos( System.nanoTime() - start );
        IO.println( &quot;duration = &quot; + duration );
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Data snippet:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>b541cd3c-60d2-4b11-b7da-7f21a5c18bea|2026-04-02T04:56:14.755410Z
a3446075-5bbd-42ab-a9b4-a154f8190900|2026-04-02T04:56:14.756789Z
9144b23b-de0e-41a8-8285-b0b0dd8ccbe6|2026-04-02T04:56:14.756800Z
…
</code></pre>
<p>On <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MacOS_Sequoia" rel="nofollow noreferrer">macOS Sequoia</a> 15.7.4, on a <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MacBook_Pro_(Apple_silicon)" rel="nofollow noreferrer">MacBook Pro</a> with <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apple_M1#M1_Pro" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Apple M1 Pro</a> chip, with built-in <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Solid-state_drive" rel="nofollow noreferrer">SSD</a> storage, running <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Java_version_history#Java_SE_26" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Java 26</a>, this takes less than one second.</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>26+35
duration = PT0.777597417S
</code></pre>
<hr />
<p>In real work, you might not want to risk losing all your data while re-writing to the same file. You likely would write to a second file, then delete the original <em>after</em> completion.</p>
<p>Also, in real work, I would add some validation such as nulls and empty strings. And I would likely use <em>JSpecify</em> annotations to enable null-checking tools.</p>

Q: How do I make an arbitrary Perl regex wholly non-capturing? A: <p>Either of these work (mostly - see 'Caveat' below):</p>
<p><code>/before(?&amp;SUBPATT)after(*ACCEPT)(?&lt;SUBPATT&gt;$sub_patt)/ </code>     or</p>
<p><code>/before(?&amp;SUBPATT)after(?(DEFINE)(?&lt;SUBPATT&gt;$sub_patt))/</code></p>
<p>The trick is to put the arbitrary sub-pattern in a capture group which is &quot;recursed into&quot; but never reached directly.   When a &quot;recursive&quot; reference is executed &quot;capture buffers set by the subpattern are not visible to the caller&quot; (<em>man perlre</em>).</p>
<p>So the sub-pattern may use arbitrary capture groups and <em>relative</em> back-references, without creating capture groups in the final result.</p>
<p>In the first method above, the <code>(*ACCEPT)</code> terminates the match before reaching the ordinary (capture group) holding the sub-pattern definition, so it never captures anything.   In the second method, the special <code>(DEFINE)</code> &quot;conditional&quot; pattern syntax is used to create a referenceable group which can be &quot;recursed into&quot; (see <em>perlre</em>).  [Thanks to <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/users/4031604/anarrayoffunctions">AnArrayOfFunctions</a> for this method!]</p>
<p><strong>Caveat:</strong> Extraneous 'undef' values appear at the end of the resulting list when the regex match is evaluated in an List context.  This is because the <code>=~</code> operator in List context returns a value for every (capture buffer) defined in the regex, including (as <em>perlre</em> sort-of says) groups in subpatterns created inside a DEFINE block.  <strong>However the <code>@{^CAPTURE}</code> array does <em>not</em> contain extra undefs</strong> (only values actually captured at the top level), so if you need to count the number of &quot;real&quot; captures, look at @{^CAPTURE} afterwards instead of assigning the match expression to an array.</p>
<pre class="lang-perl prettyprint-override"><code>#!/usr/bin/env perl
use strict; use warnings;
use Regexp::Common;
sub showarray(@) { 
  &quot;(&quot;.scalar(@_).&quot; items) &quot;
  . join(&quot;, &quot;, map{ defined($_) ? &quot;'$_'&quot; : &quot;undef&quot; } @_)  
}

my $sub_patt = $RE{balanced}{-parens=&gt;'()'};  # uses internal captures

$_ = &quot;Get this (but not this) today.&quot;;

{ print &quot;--- test 1 ---\n&quot;;
  my @a = /^([^()]*) $sub_patt ([^()]*)$/x;
  print 'Array result: ',showarray(@a),&quot;\n&quot;;
  print 'CAPTURE     : ',showarray(@{^CAPTURE}),&quot;\n&quot;;
}
# Array result: (3 items) 'Get this ', '(but not this)', ' today.'
# CAPTURE     : (3 items) 'Get this ', '(but not this)', ' today.'


{ print &quot;--- test 2 ---\n&quot;;
  my @a = /^([^()]*) (?&amp;SUBPATT) ([^()]*)$ (*ACCEPT) (?&lt;SUBPATT&gt;$sub_patt)/x;
  print 'Array result: ',showarray(@a),&quot;\n&quot;;
  print 'CAPTURE     : ',showarray(@{^CAPTURE}),&quot;\n&quot;;
}
# Array result: (4 items) 'Get this ', ' today.', undef, undef
# CAPTURE     : (2 items) 'Get this ', ' today.'


{ print &quot;--- test 3 ---\n&quot;;
  my @a = /^([^()]*) (?&amp;SUBPATT) ([^()]*)$ (*ACCEPT) (?(DEFINE)(?&lt;SUBPATT&gt;$sub_patt))/x;
  print 'Array result: ',showarray(@a),&quot;\n&quot;;
  print 'CAPTURE     : ',showarray(@{^CAPTURE}),&quot;\n&quot;;
}
# Array result: (4 items) 'Get this ', ' today.', undef, undef
# CAPTURE     : (2 items) 'Get this ', ' today.'

{ print &quot;--- test 4 ---\n&quot;;
  my @a = /^([^()]*) (?&amp;SUBPATT) ([^()]*)$           (?(DEFINE)(?&lt;SUBPATT&gt;$sub_patt))/x;
  print 'Array result: ',showarray(@a),&quot;\n&quot;;
  print 'CAPTURE     : ',showarray(@{^CAPTURE}),&quot;\n&quot;;
}
# Array result: (4 items) 'Get this ', ' today.', undef, undef
# CAPTURE     : (2 items) 'Get this ', ' today.'

{ print &quot;--- test 5 ---\n&quot;;
  my @a = /(?(DEFINE)(?&lt;SUBPATT&gt;$sub_patt)) ^([^()]*) (?&amp;SUBPATT) ([^()]*)$ /x;
  print 'Array result: ',showarray(@a),&quot;\n&quot;;
  print 'CAPTURE     : ',showarray(@{^CAPTURE}),&quot;\n&quot;;
}
# Array result: (4 items) undef, undef, 'Get this ', ' today.'
# CAPTURE     : (4 items) undef, undef, 'Get this ', ' today.'
</code></pre>
